1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,314
Welcome to lesson eighteen, and to level four.

2
00:00:03,414 --> 00:00:13,360
Up to now you've learned what each object does. Now you'll learn how Kubernetes actually works inside, so you can reason about any behavior or failure from first principles.

3
00:00:14,710 --> 00:00:17,504
Four principles explain almost everything.

4
00:00:17,604 --> 00:00:29,934
Everything is an API object stored in etcd. Components never talk to each other directly; they only read and write objects through the API server. You write the spec, and controllers write the status.

5
00:00:30,034 --> 00:00:38,460
And controllers run reconciliation loops that react to the current state, not to individual events. So if an event is missed, the next loop catches up.

6
00:00:39,810 --> 00:00:43,148
Every request goes through a pipeline in the API server.

7
00:00:43,248 --> 00:00:57,994
Authentication, audit, priority and fairness, and authorization. Then mutating admission, which can change the object, for example to inject a sidecar. Then schema validation, then validating admission, such as Pod Security.

8
00:00:58,094 --> 00:01:06,888
Finally it's stored in etcd, and everyone watching is notified. An admission webhook that's down can block Pod creation across the whole cluster.

9
00:01:08,238 --> 00:01:10,056
A few mechanics matter.

10
00:01:10,156 --> 00:01:17,270
Every object has a resource version. An update based on an old version fails with a conflict, so no change is lost.

11
00:01:17,370 --> 00:01:26,148
Generation and observed generation tell you whether a controller has processed the latest spec. Server-side apply tracks which tool owns which field.

12
00:01:26,248 --> 00:01:32,306
And clients don't poll. They open watches, and controllers keep local caches called informers.

13
00:01:33,656 --> 00:01:39,554
etcd holds all cluster state. Lose it without a backup and you lose the cluster's configuration.

14
00:01:39,654 --> 00:01:43,408
Secrets are stored readable unless encryption at rest is configured.

15
00:01:43,508 --> 00:01:55,278
etcd members agree on every write using the Raft algorithm, and a write needs a majority. Three members survive one failure, five survive two. Four is no better than three, so always use an odd number.

16
00:01:55,378 --> 00:01:59,836
If quorum is lost, nothing can change, but running Pods keep running.

17
00:02:01,186 --> 00:02:09,644
A controller is a loop: read the desired state, observe the actual state, take one step toward the desired state, and write the status.

18
00:02:09,744 --> 00:02:17,466
A Deployment doesn't create Pods directly. A chain of independent controllers does, connected only through API objects.

19
00:02:17,566 --> 00:02:30,152
Dependents point to their owners, so the garbage collector cleans them up. Finalizers make deletion wait for cleanup. And leader election, using Lease objects, ensures only one copy of a controller acts at a time.

20
00:02:31,502 --> 00:02:37,144
The scheduler only decides. It writes the node name on the Pod, and never starts anything.

21
00:02:37,244 --> 00:02:51,254
The kubelet on each node watches for Pods assigned to it. It asks the container runtime to create a sandbox, held by a tiny pause container, calls the network plugin to get an IP, mounts volumes, starts the containers, and runs probes.

22
00:02:51,354 --> 00:02:58,212
It also sends a heartbeat by renewing a Lease about every ten seconds. When that stops, the node is marked not ready.

23
00:02:59,562 --> 00:03:01,060
Here's the full journey.

24
00:03:01,160 --> 00:03:14,578
kubectl sends the request, the API server runs its pipeline, and the Deployment is stored. The Deployment controller creates a ReplicaSet, which creates Pods. The scheduler binds them, and the kubelet starts them.

25
00:03:14,678 --> 00:03:22,272
When probes pass, the Pod becomes ready, the endpoint controller adds it to the Service, and kube-proxy updates the rules.

26
00:03:22,372 --> 00:03:28,990
Each step is a separate component. If one is broken, the chain stops exactly there, and that's where troubleshooting should look.

27
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:37,966
In a highly available control plane, API servers are stateless, so all of them serve traffic behind a load balancer.

28
00:03:38,066 --> 00:03:42,668
The scheduler and controller manager run everywhere, but only the leader acts.

29
00:03:42,768 --> 00:03:49,530
And etcd needs three or more members in separate failure zones. Managed services run all of this for you.

30
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:58,314
In the lab, you'll watch kubectl's HTTP requests and open a raw watch stream, exactly like a controller.

31
00:03:58,414 --> 00:04:07,528
You'll trigger a conflict, read a Secret directly from etcd to see why encryption matters, and play the role of a controller by removing a finalizer yourself.

32
00:04:08,878 --> 00:04:10,040
Let's recap.

33
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:15,382
Everything goes through the API server, and only the API server talks to etcd.

34
00:04:15,482 --> 00:04:24,068
Controllers are level-triggered loops chained together through objects. The scheduler binds, and the kubelet runs containers through standard interfaces.

35
00:04:24,168 --> 00:04:29,618
Next, you'll learn to install, upgrade, back up and secure clusters in production.
